# Castle CRM scheduled work — /etc/cron.d/castle-crm
#
# install.sh writes this file with the placeholders filled in:
#   __CC_USER__   the user the web server runs as, e.g. www-data
#   __CC_PHP__    the PHP CLI binary, e.g. /usr/bin/php8.3
#   __CC_PATH__   the checkout, e.g. /var/www/castle-crm
#
# EVERY MINUTE, and that is not a typo. cron.php works to a wall-clock budget
# and stops cleanly when it runs out; a lock file stops two ticks overlapping.
# Automation delays, nurture sequences, reminders and the manager digest are
# all measured in minutes, so a five-minute tick would make every one of them
# late by up to five.
#
# It runs as the web server's user, not as root: everything it writes —
# storage/logs, storage/cache, storage/exports, a backup — is written by the
# application in the normal course of a request too, and a root-owned file in
# one of those directories is a permission error the next time a web request
# touches it.
#
# Output goes nowhere on purpose. cron.php logs to storage/logs/ and writes a
# heartbeat the health page watches (Settings → System health), which is a
# better record than a mail spool nobody reads. A silent cron failure is the
# most damaging failure mode this application has, and the health page is what
# makes it loud.
SHELL=/bin/sh
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin
MAILTO=""

* * * * * __CC_USER__ __CC_PHP__ __CC_PATH__/cron.php >/dev/null 2>&1
